        "source":  "InfoWorld",
        "url":  "https://www.infoworld.com/article/4198556/where-the-real-competition-is-in-ai.html",
        "date":  "2026-07-20",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Matt Asay argues that major AI companies are open-sourcing foundational protocols like MCP and A2A not from altruism but to shift competitive advantage away from frontier models toward platform infrastructure. He contends that individual models face constant commoditization through weekly leaderboard changes, while the real competitive moat emerges from becoming the ecosystem where AI-assisted work naturally accumulates ΓÇö similar to how GitHub dominated despite Git being free. The companies giving away models and protocols are competing to own the platform layer.",
        "execSummary":  "The strategic insight here is that AI model quality is a temporary moat ΓÇö the durable competitive advantage is platform lock-in through data gravity, workflow integration, and ecosystem accumulation. For enterprise leaders, this reframes vendor selection: the question is not which model scores best today but which platform will own your workflows and data in three years. Open-sourcing of protocols like MCP is a land-grab strategy, not a public good, and enterprises that adopt these protocols are implicitly choosing an ecosystem. The practical implication is that enterprise AI strategy must be evaluated as a platform decision with long-term switching costs, not a series of point-solution tool purchases.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
        "imageUrl":  "https://www.infoworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/4198556-0-37284800-1784569559-shutterstock_347074193.jpg"
