        "summary":  "Machine learning is compressing pharmaceutical R\u0026D timelines by enabling researchers to computationally evaluate vast molecular possibilities and prioritize drug candidates more efficiently. Companies like AstraZeneca are using AI prediction systems to create tighter feedback loops that reduce failed experiments and surface previously untreatable disease targets. The field is advancing toward de novo protein design where AI generates entirely new sequences from scratch, though safety prediction and standardized training datasets remain unsolved challenges.",
        "execSummary":  "AI-accelerated drug discovery illustrates the broader enterprise AI pattern: the highest-value applications are not productivity tools layered on top of existing workflows but fundamental redesigns of core processes enabled by proprietary domain data. For enterprise AI strategists, the pharma use case is a reference model for how AI shifts from augmentation to transformation when coupled with proprietary data assets. Customer-support leaders can draw a direct parallel: the most durable AI advantage will come from models trained on proprietary interaction and resolution data, not generic models applied to generic workflows. Even in pharma ΓÇö a field with massive AI investment and high motivation ΓÇö safety prediction and data standardization challenges remain unsolved, a reminder that transformative AI timelines are longer than headlines suggest.",
